Creating a JMS consumer client with WebLogic from RabbitMQ using C# is not a straightforward process, mainly because JMS (Java Message Service) is part of the Java ecosystem, and RabbitMQ is based on the AMQP (Advanced Message Queuing Protocol) standard. However, it is possible to make them communicate with each other.

To achieve this, you'll need to follow these general steps:

1. Set up the JMS provider in WebLogic and configure its connection factory.

2. Create a RabbitMQ WebSocket server that receives incoming messages from the WebLogic server.

3. Set up a C# RabbitMQ WebSocket client to communicate with the RabbitMQ WebSocket server.

4. Create the C# consumer client.

Here's a high-level example of how to create a JMS consumer client with WebLogic from RabbitMQ using C#:

1. Set up the WebLogic JMS Provider:

WebLogic JMS can be set up by creating a JMS module, connection factory, and a queue in the Admin Console. For detailed steps on configuring the JMS provider, follow the official WebLogic documentation: https://docs.oracle.com/en/middleware/fusion-middleware/weblogic-server/12.2.1.3/jmshk/index.html

2. Create a RabbitMQ WebSocket Server

You'll need a WebSocket server to allow communication from the WebLogic to RabbitMQ. We'll use Node.js to create the RabbitMQ WebSocket server.

First, install the 'websocket' and 'amqplib' packages:

```bash
npm install websocket amqplib
```

Next, create a file called "rmq-websocket.js" and add the following code:

```javascript
const WebSocketServer = require('websocket').server;
const http = require('http');
const amqp = require('amqplib/callback_api');

const server = http.createServer((request, response) => {});
server.listen(8081, () => {});
const amqpUrl = 'amqp://guest:guest@localhost';

const wsServer = new WebSocketServer({ httpServer: server });
wsServer.on('request', (request) => {
  const connection = request.accept(null, request.origin);
  const q = 'myQueue';

  connection.on('message', (message) => {
    amqp.connect(amqpUrl, (error0, amqpConnection) => {
      if (error0) throw error0;

      amqpConnection.createChannel((error1, channel) => {
        if (error1) throw error1;

        channel.assertQueue(q, { durable: false });
        channel.sendToQueue(q, Buffer.from(message.utf8Data));
      });
    });
  });

  connection.on('close', () => {});
});
```

Start the RabbitMQ WebSocket server using the following command:

```bash
node rmq-websocket.js
```

3. Set up C# RabbitMQ WebSocket Client:

Create a C# project and install the RabbitMQ.Client, WebSocket4Net, and Newtonsoft.Json NuGet packages:

```bash
dotnet add package RabbitMQ.Client
dotnet add package WebSocket4Net
dotnet add package Newtonsoft.Json
```

4. Create the C# Consumer Client:

Create a Consumer.cs file in the C# project and add the following code:

```csharp
using System;
using RabbitMQ.Client;
using RabbitMQ.Client.Events;
using WebSocket4Net;

class Consumer
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var factory = new ConnectionFactory() { Uri = new Uri("ws://localhost:8081") };
        using(var connection = factory.CreateConnection())
        {
            using(var channel = connection.CreateModel())
            {
                channel.QueueDeclare(queue: "myQueue",
                                     durable: false,
                                     exclusive: false,
                                     autoDelete: false,
                                     arguments: null);

                var consumer = new EventingBasicConsumer(channel);
                consumer.Received += (model, ea) =>
                {
                    var body = ea.Body.ToArray();
                    var message = Encoding.UTF8.GetString(body);
                    Console.WriteLine("[x] Received {0}", message);
                };

                channel.BasicConsume(queue: "myQueue",
                                     autoAck: true,
                                     consumer: consumer);

                Console.WriteLine("Press [enter] to exit.");
                Console.ReadLine();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Run the C# project using the following command:

```bash
dotnet run
```

Now, you have a C# consumer client to receive messages from WebLogic via RabbitMQ. Messages sent to the JMS queue in WebLogic will be passed through RabbitMQ and received by the C# consumer client.

