Styled Components is a popular library that allows you to write CSS inside your JavaScript code using a unique syntax called "tagged template literals". Here are the steps to use Styled Components in a React project:

1. Install the styled-components library by running the following command in your terminal:

   ```
   npm install styled-components
   ```

2. Import the styled-components library in the component where you wish to use it:

   ```javascript
   import styled from 'styled-components';
   ```

3. To create a styled component, you can call the `styled` function and pass in the name of the HTML element or any React component you want to style, followed by a template literal. Inside the template literal, you can write your CSS styles as you normally would:

   ```javascript
   const Button = styled.button`
     color: white;
     background-color: blue;
     border: none;
     padding: 10px 20px;
     border-radius: 5px;
     font-size: 1.2rem;
     &:hover {
       cursor: pointer;
       background-color: darkblue;
     }
   `;
   ```

4. You can now use the `Button` component in your JSX code just like any other React component:

   ```javascript
   function MyComponent() {
     return (
       <div>
         <h1>Welcome to my page</h1>
         <Button>Click me</Button>
       </div>
     );
   }
   ```

5. You can also pass in props to your styled components and use them to dynamically style the component:

   ```javascript
   const Button = styled.button`
     color: ${(props) => (props.primary ? 'white' : 'black')};
     background-color: ${(props) => (props.primary ? 'blue' : 'white')};
     border: none;
     padding: 10px 20px;
     border-radius: 5px;
     font-size: 1.2rem;
     &:hover {
       cursor: pointer;
       background-color: ${(props) => (props.primary ? 'darkblue' : 'lightgrey')};
     }
   `;
   ```

   You can then pass in a `primary` prop to the `Button` component to change its styling:

   ```javascript
   function MyComponent() {
     return (
       <div>
         <h1>Welcome to my page</h1>
         <Button primary>Click me</Button>
         <Button>Cancel</Button>
       </div>
     );
   }
   ```

   The first `Button` component will have a blue background and white text, while the second `Button` component will have a white background and black text.

You can use the CSS reset techniques inside the `styled-components` library to reset all styles in a React project. One way to do this is by creating a `GlobalStyle` component in `styled-components` and then adding reset styles to it. Here are the steps to do this:

1. Import the `createGlobalStyle` function from `styled-components`:

   ```javascript
   import { createGlobalStyle } from 'styled-components';
   ```

2. Create a new component called `GlobalStyle` using the `createGlobalStyle` function, and add your reset styles inside the backticks:

   ```javascript
   const GlobalStyle = createGlobalStyle`
     * {
       box-sizing: border-box;
       margin: 0;
       padding: 0;
     }

     body {
       font-family: sans-serif;
     }
   `;
   ```

   In this example, the `*` selector sets the box-sizing property to border-box and sets the margin and padding of all elements to 0. The `body` selector sets the font-family of the entire page to the sans-serif font stack.

3. Import the `GlobalStyle` component at the top level of your application and render it:

   ```javascript
   import { GlobalStyle } from './components/GlobalStyle';

   function App() {
     return (
       <div>
         <GlobalStyle />
         <h1>Welcome to my app</h1>
         <p>This is some text in my app.</p>
       </div>
     );
   }
   ```

   This will apply the global styles to all elements in your application, including all the elements inside the `App` component.

4. You can also use the `styled-components` library to create reusable components with consistent styles across your application, while still being able to take advantage of the power and flexibility of CSS. Here's an example of a `Button` component created with `styled-components` that uses the reset styles defined in the `GlobalStyle` component:

   ```javascript
   const Button = styled.button`
     color: white;
     background-color: blue;
     border: none;
     padding: 10px 20px;
     border-radius: 5px;
     font-size: 1.2rem;
     &:hover {
       cursor: pointer;
       background-color: darkblue;
     }
   `;
   ```

   By creating a `Button` component with `styled-components`, you can easily apply consistent styles to all the buttons in your application, without having to write repetitive CSS code.

