tableCaptionProperty.Value <<= true; // добавляем название таблицы.

PropertyValue tableOrientationProperty;
tableOrientationProperty.Name = "Orientation";
tableOrientationProperty.Value <<= text::TableOrientation::BOTTOM_TO_TOP_LEFT_TO_RIGHT; // ориентирование таблицы.

PropertyValue tableCellPaddingProperty;
tableCellPaddingProperty.Name = "CellPadding";
tableCellPaddingProperty.Value <<= 50; // отступы в ячейках таблицы.

PropertyValue tableHeaderRowCountProperty;
tableHeaderRowCountProperty.Name = "HeaderRowCount";
tableHeaderRowCountProperty.Value <<= 1; // количество строк заголовка таблицы.

// Добавляем все свойства в массив PropertyValue.
Sequence<PropertyValue> tableProps = {
    tableBorderProperty,
    tableWidthProperty,
    tableCaptionProperty,
    tableOrientationProperty,
    tableCellPaddingProperty,
    tableHeaderRowCountProperty
};

// Устанавливаем все свойства таблицы с помощью массива PropertyValue.
xTableProps->setPropertyValues(
    OUString::createFromAscii("Table"), // свойство таблицы.
    tableProps // массив свойств.
);
```

В этом примере переменная `xTable` представляет объект таблицы в LibreOffice, а `xTableProps` - это объект `XPropertySet` интерфейса текстового документа, представляющий свойства таблицы. Вы создаете объект `PropertyValue` для каждого свойства таблицы и добавляете его в массив `tableProps`. Затем вы вызываете метод `setPropertyValues` интерфейса `XPropertySet` для установки всех свойств таблицы одновременно, используя `Table` в качестве имени свойства таблицы. Свойства, которые устанавливаются в этом примере, это только некоторые из свойств таблицы, которые можно установить с помощью массива `PropertyValue`. Будьте осторожны, при изменении всех свойств таблицы, некоторые из них могут перекрываться и приводить к нежелательному результату.

To sort subdocuments in a mongoose model, you can use the `sort()` method chained after the `populate()` method. Here's an example:

Suppose you have a mongoose model for `Author` with a subdocument array `books`:

```js
const authorSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
  name: String,
  books: [{ title: String, year: Number }]
});

const Author = mongoose.model('Author', authorSchema);
```

To sort the books array of an author by year, you can do the following:

```js
Author.findById(authorId)
  .populate({ path: 'books', options: { sort: { year: 1 } } })
  .exec((err, author) => {
    if (err) return console.error(err);
    console.log(author.books);
  });
```

In this example, we use the `populate()` method to fetch the author with the given `authorId`, and then sort the `books` subdocument array by `year` in ascending order (i.e., from oldest to newest). The resulting `author.books` property will be an array of books sorted by year. 

You can also sort in descending order by changing the sort value to `-1`:

```js
.populate({ path: 'books', options: { sort: { year: -1 } } })
```

Another way to sort a subdocument in Mongoose is to use the `aggregate()` method. Here's an example:

```js
Author.aggregate([
  { $match: { _id: authorId } },
  { $unwind: '$books'},
  { $sort: { 'books.year': 1 } },
  { $group: { _id: '$_id', books: { $push: '$books' } } }
]).exec((err, author) => {
  if (err) return console.error(err);
  console.log(author[0].books);
});
```

In this example, we use the `aggregate()` method to sort the `books` subdocument array of an author with the given `authorId`. The four stages in the pipeline are as follows:

1. `$match`: This filters the documents in the collection to only return the one with the matching `_id`.
2. `$unwind`: This deconstructs the `books` array into separate documents for each book, so that we can sort them individually.
3. `$sort`: This sorts the documents by the `year` property of the `books` subdocument array in ascending order (i.e., from oldest to newest).
4. `$group`: This groups the documents back into one document, and uses the `$push` operator to create a new `books` array containing all the sorted books.

The resulting `author[0].books` property will be an array of books sorted by year. Note that we use `[0]` to access the first element in the `author` array, since the `aggregate()` method always returns an array even if it contains only one document.

To sort a subdocument using the `find()` method in Mongoose, you can use the `$sort` operator with the dot notation to specify the field you want to sort on. Here's an example:

```js
Author.findById(authorId)
  .select('books')
  .lean()
  .populate('books.author', 'name') // assume we have referenced authors
  .exec((err, author) => {
    if (err) return console.error(err);
    author.books = author.books.sort((a, b) => a.year - b.year);
    console.log(author.books);
  });
```

In this example, we use the `find()` method to fetch the author with the given `authorId`. We then chain the following:

1. `select`: This selects only the `books` subdocument array to reduce the amount of data being fetched from the database.
2. `lean`: This returns plain JavaScript objects instead of Mongoose documents, so that we can modify the `books` array more easily.
3. `populate`: This populates the `books.author` field with the `name` property of the referenced `Author` model. 

After executing the query, we use the `sort()` method to sort the `books` subdocument array by `year` in ascending order (i.e., from oldest to newest). The resulting `author.books` property will be an array of books sorted by year. Note that since we are using the `lean()` method, we cannot use the `sort()` method directly on the `books` array but can access its native Array.prototype.

