To put data in local storage, you can use the `window.localStorage.setItem(key, value)` method, where the key is a string, and value is the data that needs to be stored. Make sure to stringify your data if it's an object, as local storage only accepts strings.

To retrieve data from local storage, use the `window.localStorage.getItem(key)` method.

Here's an example using a functional React component:

```javascript
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

function App() {
  const [data, setData] = useState(null);

  // Function to store data in local storage
  const storeData = () => {
    const someData = { key: 'value' };
    window.localStorage.setItem('myData', JSON.stringify(someData));
  };

  // Function to retrieve data from local storage
  const getData = () => {
    const storedData = window.localStorage.getItem('myData');
    if (storedData) {
      setData(JSON.parse(storedData));
    }
  };

  // Effect to load data from local storage when component mounts
  useEffect(() => {
    getData();
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      {data && (
        <p>
          Data from local storage: {data.key}
        </p>
      )}
      <button onClick={storeData}>Store data in local storage</button>
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

In this example, we have created functions `storeData` to save data to local storage and `getData` to retrieve data from local storage. We're using the `useState` hook to manage data state and the `useEffect` hook to retrieve data from local storage when the component mounts.

