Here is an example of how to load the preloader div first before other elements using JavaScript:

1. First, add a preloader div to your HTML file. You can style this div to appear as a loader.

```html
<div id="preloader">Loading...</div>
```

2. Then, add the CSS to hide the other elements until they are loaded.

```css
body {
  visibility: hidden;
}
```

3. Use JavaScript to show the preloader div first and hide it after all the elements are loaded.

```js
window.addEventListener('load', function() {
  var preloader = document.getElementById('preloader');
  preloader.style.display = 'none';
  document.body.style.visibility = 'visible';
});
```

With this code, the preloader div will be displayed when the page is loading and will be hidden after all the elements are loaded. The other elements will only become visible once the preloader is hidden.

You can load the preloader div before the body builds by adding the preloader div as the first child of the body tag. Then, you can use the `window.onload` event to hide the preloader and show the body content after all the assets have been loaded. Here is an example:

1. Add the preloader div as the first child of the body tag:

```html
<body>
  <div id="preloader">
    <!-- add your preloader content here -->
  </div>

  <!-- your other content here -->
</body>
```

2. Add the following CSS to hide the body content until the preloader is hidden:

```css
body {
  visibility: hidden;
}
```

3. Add the following JavaScript to hide the preloader and show the body content after all the assets have been loaded:

```js
window.onload = function() {
  var preloader = document.getElementById('preloader');
  preloader.style.display = 'none';
  document.body.style.visibility = 'visible';
}
```

This code will ensure that the preloader is displayed before the body content and will be hidden once all the assets have been loaded. The body content will only be visible once the preloader is hidden.

I'm assuming you mean how to draw the entire screen at once instead of progressively rendering elements. This can be achieved by using the `requestAnimationFrame` method in a loop to draw all the elements at once. Here is an example:

```js
function drawScreen() {
  // draw all your elements here

  requestAnimationFrame(drawScreen); // loop until stopped
}

drawScreen(); // start the loop
```

This code will continuously draw all the elements on the screen until the loop is stopped. By using `requestAnimationFrame`, the loop will be synchronized with the browser's rendering engine, which can help prevent flickering and improve performance. 

You can add conditions inside the loop to stop the loop when the entire screen is drawn, or use other methods such as `setTimeout` or `setInterval` to control the frequency of drawing. But keep in mind that this technique can cause a CPU intensive rendering process, so it is usually better to use progressive rendering instead.

