To create a CSS fade in effect, you can use the following steps:

1. Start by setting the opacity of the element to 0:

```css
.fade-in {
  opacity: 0;
}
```

2. Add a transition property to the element:

```css
.fade-in {
  opacity: 0;
  transition: opacity 0.5s ease-in-out; /* Add any transition property you want */
}
```

3. Add a class to the element when you want it to fade in. You can do this using JavaScript or using pseudo-classes like `:hover` or `:focus`.

```css
.fade-in.fade-in-active {
  opacity: 1;
}
```

4. To trigger the fade in effect with JavaScript, you can add the `fade-in-active` class to the element after a specified amount of time using `setTimeout` or add/remove classes with JavaScript based on scroll or other events.

Here's an example of how to use `setTimeout` to add the `fade-in-active` class after 1000ms:

```js
setTimeout(function(){
    document.querySelector('.fade-in').classList.add('fade-in-active');
}, 1000)
```

Alternatively, you can also use CSS animations to achieve a fade in effect. Here's an example:

```css
.fade-in {
  animation-name: fadeIn;
  animation-duration: 0.5s;
  animation-timing-function: ease-in-out;
  animation-fill-mode: forwards; /* Keeps the element visible after the animation ends */
  opacity: 0;
}

@keyframes fadeIn {
  from {
    opacity: 0;
  }
  to {
    opacity: 1;
  }
}
```

With this method, you don't need to add a class to the element with JavaScript - the animation will trigger automatically when the element is loaded.

To add a `loading.gif` to all images while they are loading, you can follow these steps:

1. Add the `loading.gif` image to your HTML file.

```html
<img src="loading.gif" alt="loading" id="loader">
```

2. Add the `loading` class to all the images on your webpage that you want to show the `loading.gif` image over.

```html
<img src="image.jpg" alt="image" class="loading">
```

3. Hide all the images by default using CSS. We will display them only when they are fully loaded.

```css
.loading {
  display: none;
}
```

4. Use JavaScript to change the `src` attribute of each image to trigger the `onload` event.

```js
var images = document.querySelectorAll('.loading');
images.forEach(function(img) {
  var loader = document.getElementById('loader');
  loader.style.display = 'block';

  img.onload = function() {
    loader.style.display = 'none';
    img.style.display = 'block';
  }

  img.src = img.getAttribute('data-src');
});
```

Here, we first select all the elements with the `loading` class using `document.querySelectorAll()`. Then, we display the `loading.gif` image by setting its `display` CSS property to `block`. Next, we set the `onload` event handler function for each image. When the image is fully loaded, the `onload` event will trigger and hide the `loading.gif` image and display the fully loaded image.

Finally, we set the `src` attribute of each image to a `data-src` attribute to only trigger the loading when the script is running. This helps avoid unnecessary network requests. You can also add a fallback image in case the image cannot be loaded by setting the `onerror` event.

