To achieve this functionality, you can make use of Ant Design's `Tabs` and `Button` components. To hide the tab selector, you can customize the styling. To select the next tab, you can create a state variable and update it when the button is clicked. Here's the implementation:

```javascript
import React, { useState } from "react";
import { Tabs, Button } from "antd";
import "./App.css";

const { TabPane } = Tabs;

const App = () => {
  const [activeTab, setActiveTab] = useState("1");

  const handleButtonClick = () => {
    if (activeTab === "3") {
      return;
    } else {
      setActiveTab((prevState) => (parseInt(prevState) + 1).toString());
    }
  };

  return (
    <div className="App">
      <Tabs activeKey={activeTab} renderTabBar={() => null}>
        <TabPane tab="" key="1">
          <div>Content of Tab 1</div>
          <Button onClick={handleButtonClick}>Next</Button>
        </TabPane>
        <TabPane tab="" key="2">
          <div>Content of Tab 2</div>
          <Button onClick={handleButtonClick}>Next</Button>
        </TabPane>
        <TabPane tab="" key="3">
          <div>Content of Tab 3</div>
          <Button onClick={handleButtonClick} disabled>
            Next
          </Button>
        </TabPane>
      </Tabs>
    </div>
  );
};

export default App;
```

To hide the tab selector, you can use a CSS file, as suggested in the code:

```css
/* ./App.css */
.App {
  width: 500px;
  margin: 20px auto;
}
```

Here's how it works:

1. Import the necessary components and hooks from `react` and `antd`.
2. Create a state variable `activeTab` to hold the current active tab and set its default value to "1".
3. Define a `handleButtonClick` function that increments the value of `activeTab` until it reaches the last tab. In this case, disabled the Next button if it is the last tab.
4. Use `Tabs` component with the `activeKey` prop set to `activeTab` and `renderTabBar={() => null}` to hide the tab selector.
5. Inside `Tabs`, create 3 `TabPane` components with empty `tab` prop.
6. Add your content and use `Button` component with an `onClick` event that invokes `handleButtonClick` to navigate to the next tab.

To achieve this, you can create a state variable that holds a list of components. When the button is pressed, update the state by appending a new component to the list. You can use Ant Design's `Button`, `Form`, and `Input` components for this purpose. Here's the implementation:

```javascript
import React, { useState } from "react";
import { Button, Form, Input } from "antd";

const CustomComponent = () => (
  <div>
    <span>Some Text</span>
    <Input placeholder="Enter text" style={{ marginLeft: 10 }} />
  </div>
);

const App = () => {
  const [components, setComponents] = useState([]);

  const handleButtonClick = () => {
    setComponents((prevState) => [
      ...prevState,
      <CustomComponent key={prevState.length} />,
    ]);
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <Form>
        <div>
          {components.map((component) => (
            <Form.Item key={component.key}>{component}</Form.Item>
          ))}
        </div>
        <Form.Item>
          <Button type="primary" onClick={handleButtonClick}>
            Add Component
          </Button>
        </Form.Item>
      </Form>
    </div>
  );
};

export default App;
```

Here's how it works:

1. Import the required components and hooks from `react` and `antd`.
2. Create a state variable `components` which will contain the list of custom components.
3. Define a function `handleButtonClick` that appends a new `CustomComponent` to the list of components and updates the state.
4. Create a `CustomComponent` that displays some text and an `antd` `Input`.
5. Use the `Form` component to wrap all the custom components and the button.
6. Map the list of components and display them as `Form.Item` elements.
7. Add a `Button` component with an `onClick` event that invokes `handleButtonClick`, which will add a new `CustomComponent` to the form.

Now, every time you press the button, a new `CustomComponent` will be added below the existing ones.

