I can provide you with a general outline and hints, but implementing a moving average filter for an .mp3 file in C++ requires some understanding of audio processing libraries and a significant amount of code that goes beyond the scope of this platform. Here is a suggested approach to get started:

1. Choose an audio processing library for C++. One option is using libsndfile (http://www.mega-nerd.com/libsndfile/) to handle audio file I/O, and mpg123 (https://www.mpg123.de/) for decoding .mp3 files.

2. Install the libraries on your system and include them in your project.

3. Write a function to read audio samples from an .mp3 file, decode them, and store them in a vector or array.

4. Implement the moving average filter function. The basic idea is to take a window of the samples and average them out. Here's a simple example for a 1D array/vector:

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>

std::vector<double> moving_average_filter(const std::vector<double>& input, int window_size) {
    std::vector<double> output;
    int input_size = input.size();

    for (int i = 0; i < input_size; i++) {
        double sum = 0.0;
        int count = 0;
        for (int j = i - window_size; j <= i + window_size; j++) {
            if (j >= 0 && j < input_size) {
                sum += input[j];
                count++;
            }
        }
        output.push_back(sum / count);
    }

    return output;
}
```

5. Apply the moving average filter to the audio samples.

6. Write the filtered audio samples back to a new .wav file.

7. Compile your code with the proper library linking flags. An example using g++:

```bash
g++ your_code.cpp -o your_program -lsndfile -lmpg123
```

