Ah, I see. In that case, you can use the CSS `max-height` property to achieve the animation effect. Here's how:

```css
#btn-category-group {
  max-height: 420px;
  transition: max-height 0.5s ease-out;
}
#btn-category-group.expanded {
  max-height: none;
}
```

With this CSS, the `max-height` of `#btn-category-group` is initially set to `420px` so that it's visible. Then, when you add the `.expanded` class, the `max-height` is set to `none`, which means it will expand to fit the content inside the div.

And here's how you can use jQuery to add or remove the `expanded` class:

```javascript
$("#btn-category-group").addClass("expanded");
```

When you add the `.expanded` class to `#btn-category-group`, the `max-height` property will smoothly transition from `420px` to `none` in `0.5s`, which creates the animation effect.

