To change the language of all Ant Design components to Spanish in a React project, follow these steps:

1. Install the required dependencies:
   You will need `@ant-design/react` and `moment` packages. You can install them by running:
   ```
   npm install antd moment
   ```
   Or if you are using Yarn:
   ```
   yarn add antd moment
   ```

2. Import the required components, styles, and i18n language:
   In your root component file (usually `App.js` or `index.js`), import the Ant Design styles, Layout component, and Spanish locale as follows:
   ```javascript
   import 'antd/dist/antd.css'; // Import Ant Design styles
   import { Layout } from 'antd';
   import esES from 'antd/lib/locale/es_ES'; // Import Spanish locale
   import moment from 'moment';
   import 'moment/locale/es'; // Import Moment.js Spanish locale
   ```

3. Set the default locale for Moment.js and Ant Design:
   After importing the required packages, set the default locale for Moment.js to Spanish, and use the `ConfigProvider` component from Ant Design to set the locale for all components. Update your root component with the following code:
   ```javascript
   import React from 'react';
   import { ConfigProvider, Layout } from 'antd';
   import esES from 'antd/lib/locale/es_ES';
   import moment from 'moment';
   import 'moment/locale/es';
   import 'antd/dist/antd.css';

   moment.locale('es'); // Set Moment.js default locale to Spanish

   const App = () => {
      return (
         <ConfigProvider locale={esES}>
            <Layout>
               {/* Your app components here */}
            </Layout>
         </ConfigProvider>
      );
   };

   export default App;
   ```

With the above code, all Ant Design components in your React app will use the Spanish language. You can find more information about the `ConfigProvider` component and other language packages in the [Ant Design documentation](https://ant.design/components/config-provider/).

You can store each day's start and end times in a single state object and use a loop to generate the TimePicker components for each day. Here's a modified example:

```javascript
import React, { useState } from "react";
import { Row, Col, TimePicker } from "antd";

const TimePickerExample = () => {
  const [time, setTime] = useState({
    Monday: { startTime: "", endTime: "" },
    Tuesday: { startTime: "", endTime: "" },
    Wednesday: { startTime: "", endTime: "" },
    Thursday: { startTime: "", endTime: "" },
    Friday: { startTime: "", endTime: "" },
    Saturday: { startTime: "", endTime: "" },
    Sunday: { startTime: "", endTime: "" },
  });

  const onChangeTime = (day) => (date, dateString) => {
    setTime((prevTime) => ({
      ...prevTime,
      [day]: { startTime: dateString[0], endTime: dateString[1] },
    }));
  };

  const days = [
    "Monday",
    "Tuesday",
    "Wednesday",
    "Thursday",
    "Friday",
    "Saturday",
    "Sunday",
  ];

  return (
    <Row className="section-modal" style={{ marginTop: "1rem" }}>
      <Col
        span={24}
        style={{
          width: "90%",
          paddingLeft: "2rem",
          paddingRight: "2rem",
        }}
      >
        <p>HORARIOS DE USO:</p>

        {days.map((day) => (
          <div key={day} className="time_days">
            <p>{day}:</p>
            <TimePicker.RangePicker
              format="HH:mm"
              onChange={onChangeTime(day)}
            />
          </div>
        ))}
      </Col>
    </Row>
  );
};

export default TimePickerExample;
```

In this example, I created an array `days` containing the names of each day. Then, I mapped over the `days` array and generated a TimePicker.RangePicker component for each day. The `onChangeTime` function now takes the day as an argument, and returns another function that will properly update the state with the start and end times for that day.

