Here's an example Jena code that creates an ontology where two instances of the Person class, `p1` and `p2`, are related by the `address` and `neighborhood` object properties using a property rule. The property rule infers that `p1` and `p2` are in the same neighborhood if they have the same `address`.

```java
// Create the ontology model
OntModel model = ModelFactory.createOntologyModel();
String NS = "http://example.com/";

// Create the property rule
String rule = "[rule1: (?x <http://example.com/address> ?y) "
            + "(?z <http://example.com/address> ?y) "
            + "(?x != ?z) -> "
            + "(?x <http://example.com/neighborhood> ?z), "
            + "(?z <http://example.com/neighborhood> ?x)]";
Reasoner reasoner = new GenericRuleReasoner(Rule.parseRules(rule));

// Set the reasoner for the ontology model
InfModel infModel = ModelFactory.createInfModel(reasoner, model);

// Create two instances of the Person class
Individual p1 = infModel.createIndividual(NS + "p1", model.getOntClass(NS + "Person"));
Individual p2 = infModel.createIndividual(NS + "p2", model.getOntClass(NS + "Person"));

// Create the object properties
ObjectProperty address = infModel.createObjectProperty(NS + "address");
ObjectProperty neighborhood = infModel.createObjectProperty(NS + "neighborhood");

// Add the object properties to the model
model.add(address, RDF.type, OWL.ObjectProperty);
model.add(neighborhood, RDF.type, OWL.ObjectProperty);

// Set the address for each individual
p1.addProperty(address, "10 Main St");
p2.addProperty(address, "10 Main St");

// Query the inferred model for the neighborhood relationship
Property hasNeighborhood = infModel.createProperty(NS + "neighborhood");
StmtIterator stmt = infModel.listStatements(p1, hasNeighborhood, (RDFNode)null);
while (stmt.hasNext()) {
    Statement st = stmt.nextStatement();
    Individual neighborhoodOfP1 = (Individual)st.getObject();
    System.out.println("p1 and "+ neighborhoodOfP1.getURI() + " are in the same neighborhood");
}
```

In this example, the property rule is created using the `Rule.parseRules()` method, which takes a string representation of the rule as input. The rule has two conditions that check if two persons have the same address, and then infers that they are in the same neighborhood. The `GenericRuleReasoner` class is used to create a reasoner instance from the rule. The reasoner is then attached to the ontology model using `ModelFactory.createInfModel()`. 

Next, two instances of the Person class are created and related by the `address` object property. The `address` and `neighborhood` properties are also added to the model. Finally, the inferred model is queried for the neighborhood relationship using the `listStatements()` method. This returns all the statements where `p1` is related to another individual by the `neighborhood` property.

