To calculate the number of similar objects in an ImGui drawlist, you can iterate through the draw commands and count the number of times a particular object is being drawn. 

For example, let's say you are drawing circles onto an ImGui drawlist. You could add a custom parameter to the circle draw command to specify the color of the circle:

```cpp
draw_list->AddCircle(ImVec2(100, 100), 50, IM_COL32(255, 0, 0, 255), 32);
draw_list->AddCircle(ImVec2(200, 100), 50, IM_COL32(0, 255, 0, 255), 32);
draw_list->AddCircle(ImVec2(300, 100), 50, IM_COL32(0, 0, 255, 255), 32);
draw_list->AddCircle(ImVec2(400, 100), 50, IM_COL32(255, 0, 0, 255), 32);
```

To count the number of red circles, you could iterate through the draw commands and count how many times the IM_COL32(255, 0, 0, 255) color is used:

```cpp
int red_circle_count = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < draw_list->CmdBuffer.Size; i++)
{
    const ImDrawCmd* cmd = &draw_list->CmdBuffer[i];
    if (cmd->UserCallback != nullptr)
    {
        // Handle custom user callbacks
    }
    else
    {
        const ImDrawIdx* idx_buffer = draw_list->IdxBuffer.Data + cmd->IdxOffset;
        for (int j = 0; j < cmd->ElemCount; j += 3)
        {
            ImDrawIdx idx0 = idx_buffer[j + 0];
            ImDrawIdx idx1 = idx_buffer[j + 1];
            ImDrawIdx idx2 = idx_buffer[j + 2];

            // Check the color of the circle being drawn
            const ImDrawVert* v0 = &draw_list->VtxBuffer[idx0];
            const ImDrawVert* v1 = &draw_list->VtxBuffer[idx1];
            const ImDrawVert* v2 = &draw_list->VtxBuffer[idx2];
            if (v0->col == IM_COL32(255, 0, 0, 255) &&
                v1->col == IM_COL32(255, 0, 0, 255) &&
                v2->col == IM_COL32(255, 0, 0, 255))
            {
                red_circle_count++;
            }
        }
    }
}

ImGui::Text("Red circles: %d", red_circle_count);
```

This code iterates through each command in the drawlist and checks the color of each vertex in the triangle. If all three vertices have the same color (in this case, IM_COL32(255, 0, 0, 255) for red circles), then the count for that color is incremented. Finally, the count is displayed using a ImGui::Text command. 

You could modify this code to count the number of other types of objects (e.g. rectangles, lines, text) by checking for their respective vertex patterns.

If you're only interested in getting the number of objects being drawn in an ImGui drawlist, you can count the number of element commands in the command buffer:

```cpp
int object_count = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < draw_list->CmdBuffer.Size; i++)
{
