Creating a speech synthesizer without using any external libraries could be quite challenging because it requires complex speech processing algorithms and access to a large database of voice samples. Instead, you might use a basic text-to-speech (TTS) program that relies on your system's built-in TTS capabilities.

Here's an example using the `SAPI` (Speech API) available on Windows:

```c
// Compile with:
// gcc -o text_to_speech text_to_speech.c -l ole32 -l sapi

#include <stdio.h>
#include <initguid.h> // Required for DEFINE_GUID initialization
#include <sapi.h>
#include <objbase.h>
#include <oleauto.h>

static ISpVoice * pVoice = NULL;

BOOL InitializeSpeech() {
    HRESULT hr;
    hr = CoInitialize(NULL);
    if (SUCCEEDED(hr)) hr = CoCreateInstance(&CLSID_SpVoice, NULL, CLSCTX_ALL, &IID_ISpVoice, (void **)&pVoice);

    return SUCCEEDED(hr);
}

void UninitializeSpeech() {
    if (pVoice) {
        ISpVoice_Release(pVoice);
        pVoice = NULL;
    }
    CoUninitialize();
}

void SpeakText(const wchar_t *text) {
    if (pVoice) ISpVoice_Speak(pVoice, text, SPF_DEFAULT, NULL);
}

int main() {
    if (InitializeSpeech()) {
        printf("Speech initialized.\n");
        SpeakText(L"Hello, this is a simple C program for text-to-speech using SAPI.");
    } else {
        printf("Failed to initialize speech.\n");
        return 1;
    }

    getchar(); // Wait for a keypress
    UninitializeSpeech();
    return 0;
}
```

This example uses the Microsoft Speech API (SAPI) available in Windows. Remember that this won't work as a cross-platform solution, and it requires the `-l ole32 -l sapi` linker flags.

You can install the SAPI library using the package manager for MinGW on Windows:

```bash
pacman -S mingw-w64-x86_64-sapi
```

Please note that this is a quite simple text-to-speech program using Windows systems' capabilities. However, creating a proper speech synthesizer natively in C without external libraries would be very complex and time-consuming.

