in the database, one for future master classes and another for past master classes. So you can use firestore to fetch the data from both collections and filter them based on the search term entered by the user. Here's one way to do it:

1. Create a new screen called "Search" where you will display the search results.

2. Add a TextInput and a Button to the Header component. The TextInput will be used to enter the search term, and the Button will be used to trigger the search.

3. When the user clicks on the search button, you can use the entered search term to fetch data from both collections in firestore using the following code:

```javascript
const [searchTerm, setSearchTerm] = useState('');
const [searchResults, setSearchResults] = useState([]);

const handleSearch = async () => {
  const futureMkSnapshot = await firebase.firestore().collection('futureMk').where('title', '>=', searchTerm).where('title', '<=', searchTerm + '\uf8ff').get();
  const pastMkSnapshot = await firebase.firestore().collection('pastMk').where('title', '>=', searchTerm).where('title', '<=', searchTerm + '\uf8ff').get();
  const futureMkData = futureMkSnapshot.docs.map(doc => ({ id: doc.id, ...doc.data() }));
  const pastMkData = pastMkSnapshot.docs.map(doc => ({ id: doc.id, ...doc.data() }));
  const results = [...futureMkData, ...pastMkData];
  setSearchResults(results);
  navigation.navigate('Search', { results });
}
```

