An exe patcher is a program that modifies an existing executable file. Here is a simple example of how to create an exe patcher in C:

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
  char *filename = argv[1];   // Get the filename of the executable to patch
  long offset = atol(argv[2]);  // Get the offset of the patch
  char patch = atoi(argv[3]);  // Get the patch value
  FILE *file = fopen(filename, "r+b");  // Open the file for read/write binary access
  if (file == NULL) {  // Check if the file was successfully opened
    printf("Unable to open file: %s\n", filename);
    return 1;  // Return an error code
  }
  fseek(file, offset, SEEK_SET);  // Move the file pointer to the offset of the patch
  fputc(patch, file); // Write the patch value to the file
  fclose(file); // Close the file
  printf("Patch applied successfully!\n");
  return 0;
}
```

This example takes three command line arguments: the filename of the executable to patch, the offset of the patch (in bytes), and the patch value. It then opens the file in read/write binary mode, seeks to the patch offset, and writes the patch value to the file. Finally, it closes the file and prints a success message.

Note that this is a simple example and does not include error checking, input validation, or support for more complex patches (such as replacing multiple bytes or inserting new code). Additionally, modifying executable files can be dangerous and should not be done without a thorough understanding of the consequences.

