The `MessageReactionRemove` event in discord.js only fires for reactions that are removed from messages that have already been cached by the bot. If the message was sent before the bot started running or if the bot's cache is cleared, the event will not fire.

Here are a couple of solutions you can try:

1. Fetch the message object when the bot starts up:
   When your bot starts up, you can fetch the message objects for the old messages that may have reactions on them, and cache them in memory. This will ensure that when a reaction is removed from an old message, the corresponding `MessageReactionRemove` event will fire.

   Example:

   ```js
   // fetch a message by its ID when the bot starts up
   const channel = await client.channels.fetch(channelId); // replace channelId with the ID of the channel
   const message = await channel.messages.fetch(messageId); // replace messageId with the ID of the message
   ```

   You can do this for all the old messages that may have reactions on them.

2. Use the raw event:
   The `raw` event is fired whenever a websocket event is received by the bot. You can use the `raw` event to listen for `MESSAGE_REACTION_REMOVE` events, which are fired whenever a reaction is removed from any message, regardless of whether it is cached by the bot or not.

   Example:

   ```js
   client.on('raw', async (event) => {
     if (event.t === 'MESSAGE_REACTION_REMOVE') {
       const { d: data } = event;
       const channel = await client.channels.fetch(data.channel_id);
       if (!channel) return;
       const message = await channel.messages.fetch(data.message_id);
       if (!message) return;
       const user = await client.users.fetch(data.user_id);
       if (!user) return;
       const reaction = message.reactions.cache.get(data.emoji.name);
       if (!reaction) return;

       client.emit('messageReactionRemove', reaction, user);
     }
   });
   ```

   This code listens for the `raw` event, and if it receives a `MESSAGE_REACTION_REMOVE` event, it fetches the channel, message, user, and reaction objects before manually emitting a `MessageReactionRemove` event. This should work for reactions on old messages as well.

