The @TransactionAttribute(TransactionAttributeType.NOT_SUPPORTED) annotation is used in Java Enterprise Edition (Java EE) applications to specify that a method should not be executed within a transaction context. This means that the method will always be executed outside of any active transaction and will not participate in any ongoing transaction.

When this annotation is applied to a method, it overrides any transactional settings defined at the class level or in the deployment descriptor.

Here's an example of how this annotation can be used:

```java
import javax.ejb.Stateless;
import javax.ejb.TransactionAttribute;
import javax.ejb.TransactionAttributeType;

@Stateless
public class UserService {
    // This method will be executed without a transaction
    @TransactionAttribute(TransactionAttributeType.NOT_SUPPORTED)
    public void deleteUser(String userId) {
        // Delete the user from the database
    }
}
```

In the above example, the `deleteUser` method in the `UserService` class is annotated with `@TransactionAttribute(TransactionAttributeType.NOT_SUPPORTED)`. This means that whenever this method is invoked, it will be executed without any transaction context.

