In this example, the rate limiter configuration uses Redis as a backend store to store the rate limiting configuration. The `rateLimiterBucket` bean is used in the API endpoint to check whether the API call should be allowed or not based on the defined rate limit.

Note: Make sure you handle the exception thrown when the rate limit is exceeded (`TooManyRequestsException` in the example) and return appropriate response status as per your application requirements.

Once you have the Spring Boot application with rate limiting implemented, you can deploy it as multiple Docker containers in a Kubernetes cluster. Kubernetes will ensure scalability and load balancing among the containers, while each container can independently interact with the Redis rate limiter backend.

Ensure you have Redis up and running and accessible to the Kubernetes cluster before deploying the application.

If you have multiple APIs and want to rate limit each of them separately, you should create a new bucket for each API request. This allows you to set different rate limits and manage them individually.

To create separate buckets for different APIs, you can modify the previous example as follows:

1. Modify the rate limiter configuration bean to accept a parameter for the API key or identifier:
```java
@Configuration
public class RateLimiterConfig {

    @Autowired
    private RedisConnectionFactory redisConnectionFactory;

    @Bean
    public Bucket rateLimiterBucket() {
        RedisBucketsBuilderFactory factory = RedisBucketsBuilderFactory.newInstance(redisConnectionFactory);
        return factory.create("my_bucket", Bandwidth.simple(100, Duration.ofMinutes(1)));
    }

    @Bean
    public Bucket apiRateLimiterBucket() {
        RedisBucketsBuilderFactory factory = RedisBucketsBuilderFactory.newInstance(redisConnectionFactory);
        return factory.create("api_bucket", Bandwidth.simple(50, Duration.ofMinutes(1)));
    }
}
```

In this example, you have defined two buckets: `rateLimiterBucket` and `apiRateLimiterBucket`, with different rate limits for different APIs. You can adjust the bandwidth values (`100` and `50` in the example) based on your specific requirements.

2. Modify the API endpoint to use the appropriate rate limiter bucket:
```java
@RestController
public class MyController {

    @Autowired
    private Bucket apiRateLimiterBucket;

    @GetMapping("/api1/rate-limited")
    public String rateLimitedApi1() {
        if (apiRateLimiterBucket.tryConsume(1)) {
            // API logic implementation
            return "API 1 call successful";
        } else {
            throw new TooManyRequestsException("Too many API 1 requests");
        }
    }

    @GetMapping("/api2/rate-limited")
