    float input = 3.14;
    int16_t output;

    // Copy the memory representation of the float value
    uint32_t temp;
    memcpy(&temp, &input, sizeof(float));

    // Extract the exponent and mantissa
    int8_t exponent = (temp >> 23) & 0xFF;
    uint32_t mantissa = temp & 0x7FFFFF;

    // Combine the exponent and mantissa
    output = (exponent + 64) << 8 | (mantissa >> 15);

    return 0;
}
```

In this modified code, the sign bit is excluded, and only the positive value of the float is converted to the IEEE-11073 format. The resulting value will be stored in the `output` variable.

Apologies for the confusion. To convert a float to 2 bytes in the IEEE-11073 format, where the exponent is 4 bits and the mantissa is 12 bits, you can make the following changes to the previous code:

```cpp
