To get the size of the data pointed to by a `ctxt`, you can use the `sizeof` operator. However, since `ctxt` is an array of a single element, you need to multiply the size by the number of elements.

Here's an example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

typedef struct {
    // struct members
} ctxt[1];

int main() {
    ctxt myStruct;

    // Get the size of the data pointed to by ctxt
    size_t size = sizeof(myStruct) * 1;

    printf("Size of ctxt: %zu bytes\n", size);

    return 0;
}
```

In this code, `myStruct` is an instance of `ctxt`, and `sizeof(myStruct)` returns the size of one element of `ctxt`. By multiplying it by `1`, you get the total size of the data pointed to by `ctxt`. Note that multiplying by `1` is optional in this case, as the result will be the same.

The `size` variable holds the size of the data, which can then be printed using `printf`.

Remember to replace the `// struct members` comment with the actual members of your `struct`.

