The "get_current_tgid" function is not a part of FreeRTOS, but it is commonly used in Linux-based operating systems. It is used to retrieve the Thread Group ID (TGID) of the currently executing thread.

In Linux, processes can be organized into thread groups where a process and all its threads belong to the same group. The TGID is a unique identifier assigned to each thread group, and it can be retrieved using the get_current_tgid function.

Here's an example of how the get_current_tgid function can be used in a Linux-based system:

```c
#include <linux/sched.h>
#include <linux/kernel.h>

pid_t get_current_tgid(void) {
    struct task_struct *task = current;

    // Traverse the parent process chain until the root of the thread group is reached
    while (task->tgid != task->pid) {
        task = task->real_parent;
    }

    // Return the TGID of the root of the thread group
    return task->tgid;
}
```

Note that this function is specific to Linux and may not be applicable in other operating systems or in the FreeRTOS environment.

In FreeRTOS, there is no concept of a Thread Group ID (TGID) like in Linux-based operating systems. FreeRTOS focuses on managing tasks rather than processes or thread groups. Each task in FreeRTOS has its own unique identifier called a task handle.

To retrieve the task handle of the currently executing task in FreeRTOS, you can use the function `xTaskGetCurrentTaskHandle()`. Here's an example implementation:

```c
#include "FreeRTOS.h"
#include "task.h"

TaskHandle_t get_current_task_handle(void) {
    return xTaskGetCurrentTaskHandle();
}
```

With this function, you can get the task handle of the currently executing task in FreeRTOS. However, please note that this task handle is only valid within the context of the FreeRTOS scheduler and cannot provide any system-level identification like a TGID in Linux.

The `init_waitqueue_head` function is used to initialize a waitqueue head. In FreeRTOS, there is no equivalent function or built-in waitqueue head concept like in Linux-based operating systems.

In FreeRTOS, task synchronization is typically achieved using task notifications, semaphores, queues, or event groups. These mechanisms provide similar functionality to waitqueues in Linux but with different implementation approaches.

If you are looking for a way to implement a waitqueue-like functionality in FreeRTOS, you can create a notification object or a semaphore, and use it to synchronize tasks, waiting and signaling as needed.

For example, to create a notification object that acts as a simple waitqueue:

```c
#include "FreeRTOS.h"
#include "task.h"

// Global notification object
TaskHandle_t waitqueue;

void initialize_waitqueue(void) {
    // Create a binary semaphore as the waitqueue
    waitqueue = xSemaphoreCreateBinary();
}

