If you are facing a CORS error while trying to fetch an image from the NASA API, it means that the server hosting the image does not allow cross-origin requests from your web application. In such cases, you cannot directly fetch the image from the client-side JavaScript code due to browser security restrictions.

One possible solution is to fetch the image from the server-side code of your React app and then provide it to the client for download. Here are the steps:

1. Create an API route or endpoint on your server that will proxy the NASA API request and fetch the image from there. This bypasses the CORS restrictions because the request is made from the server-side.

2. In your React app, call this API route or endpoint to fetch the image using `fetch`.

3. Once you receive the image on the client-side, you can provide a download link or button to allow the user to save the image.

Here's an example of how the server-side code could look like using Node.js and Express:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
const fs = require('fs');

const app = express();

app.get('/download-image', async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const imageUrl = 'https://example.com/nasa-image.jpg'; // Replace with your actual NASA image URL
    const response = await fetch(imageUrl);
    const buffer = await response.buffer();

    // Save the image to a temporary file
    const tempFilePath = '/path/to/temp/nasa-image.jpg'; // Replace with your desired temporary file path
    fs.writeFileSync(tempFilePath, buffer);

    // Send the temporary file as a response for download
    res.download(tempFilePath, 'nasa-image.jpg', (error) => {
      // Handle any errors that occurred during the download
      if (error) {
        console.error('Error downloading image:', error);
      }

      // Delete the temporary file after sending the download
      fs.unlinkSync(tempFilePath);
    });
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error fetching image:', error);
    res.status(500).send({ error: 'Error fetching image' });
  }
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server is running');
});
```

In this example, when you hit the `/download-image` route, the server will fetch the image from the NASA API, save it to a temporary file, and then send that file as a response for download. The temporary file is deleted after the download completes or if any errors occur.

