```java
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.filter.GatewayFilter;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.filter.GatewayFilterChain;
import org.springframework.web.server.ServerWebExchange;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;

public class RateLimiterFilter implements GatewayFilter {

    private static final int REFILL_RATE = 10;
    private static final int BURST_CAPACITY = 10;

    @Override
    public Mono<Void> filter(ServerWebExchange exchange, GatewayFilterChain chain) {
        RateLimiter rateLimiter = RateLimiter.create(REFILL_RATE, BURST_CAPACITY);

        // Check if there are available permits for the current request
        if (rateLimiter.tryAcquire()) {
            // If there are permits available, allow the request to proceed
            return chain.filter(exchange);
        } else {
            // If there are no permits available, reject the request with a rate limit exceeded error response
            exchange.getResponse().setStatusCode(HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS);
            return exchange.getResponse().setComplete();
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we use the `RateLimiter` class from the Guava library to implement the rate limiting logic. The `REFILL_RATE` variable represents the desired refill rate in calls per second, which is 10 in this case. The `BURST_CAPACITY` variable represents the maximum number of calls that can be burst before being rate-limited, also set to 10.

The `filter` method is invoked for each incoming request. It creates a `RateLimiter` object with the specified refill rate and burst capacity. It then checks if there are available permits for the current request using the `tryAcquire` method. If permits are available, the request is allowed to proceed by calling `chain.filter(exchange)`. If no permits are available, a rate limit exceeded response is set and returned.

