In order to properly handle special characters in SAXReader, you need to make sure that you set the character encoding of the input source correctly. This is important because different character encodings can represent special characters differently.

Here's an example of how you can set the character encoding when using SAXReader:

```
SAXReader saxReader = new SAXReader();
saxReader.setFeature("http://xml.org/sax/features/validation", true); // Enable validation if needed

// Set character encoding
InputSource inputSource = new InputSource("input.xml");
inputSource.setEncoding("UTF-8"); // Set the appropriate encoding for your input

// Parse the XML
Document document = saxReader.read(inputSource);
```

In this example, we are setting the encoding to UTF-8, which is a common character encoding that can handle a wide range of special characters. Adjust the encoding value according to your specific needs.

Once the XML is parsed, you can access the special characters through the Document object and its various methods, such as getElementByID(), getText(), etc. SAXReader will handle the decoding of the characters automatically based on the specified encoding.

