In C++, you can use the `getline` or `split` function to split a string into parts based on a delimiter. Here's an example using the `getline` function:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::string str = "Hello, how are you?";
    std::vector<std::string> parts;
    std::stringstream ss(str);
    std::string part;

    while (std::getline(ss, part, ' ')) {
        parts.push_back(part);
    }

    for (const auto& part : parts) {
        std::cout << part << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the `getline` function is used to split the string `str` into parts based on the space (' ') delimiter. Each part is then stored in the `parts` vector. Finally, the parts are printed one by one.

Alternatively, you can also utilize the `split` function from the `boost` library:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <boost/algorithm/string.hpp>

int main() {
    std::string str = "Hello, how are you?";
    std::vector<std::string> parts;

    boost::split(parts, str, boost::is_any_of(" "));

    for (const auto& part : parts) {
        std::cout << part << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the `split` function from the `boost` library is used to split the string `str` into parts based on the space (' ') delimiter. The parts are then stored in the `parts` vector and printed one by one. Note that you'll need to have the `boost` library installed and properly configured in your project to use this approach.

To split a string character by character in C++, you can iterate over the string and extract each character individually. Here's an example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::string str = "Hello";

    for (char c : str) {
        std::cout << c << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the `for` loop iterates over each character `c` in the string `str`. Each character is then printed on a new line. This allows you to split the string into its individual characters.

To assign each character of a string to its own variable in C++, you can use an array or a vector to store the characters. Here's an example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::string str = "Hello";
    std::vector<char> characters;

    for (char c : str) {
        characters.push_back(c);
    }

    for (size_t i = 0; i < characters.size(); ++i) {
        char currentChar = characters[i];
        std::cout << "Character " << i << ": " << currentChar << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, a vector called `characters` is used to store each character of the string `str` individually. The `for` loop iterates over each character `c` in the string and uses the `push_back` function to add it to the `characters` vector. Then, another `for` loop is used to access each character from the vector by index and assign it to the `currentChar` variable. Finally, the characters are printed along with their corresponding index.

Alternatively, you can use an array to store the characters instead of a vector:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::string str = "Hello";
    char characters[str.length()];

    for (size_t i = 0; i < str.length(); ++i) {
        characters[i] = str[i];
    }

    for (size_t i = 0; i < str.length(); ++i) {
        char currentChar = characters[i];
        std::cout << "Character " << i << ": " << currentChar << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
