Sure! Here's an example of a for loop in C that generates and prints every prime number between 1 and 1000:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int i, j, flag;

    // Loop through numbers from 2 to 1000
    for (i = 2; i <= 1000; i++) {
        flag = 0;

        // Check if current number (i) is divisible by any number less than it
        for (j = 2; j < i; j++) {
            if (i % j == 0) {
                flag = 1;
                break;
            }
        }

        // Print the prime number
        if (flag == 0) {
            printf("%d ", i);
        }
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This code uses nested for loops to iterate through numbers from 2 to 1000. For each number, it checks if it is divisible by any number less than itself (excluding 1). If a divisor is found, the `flag` is set to 1, indicating that the number is not prime. If no divisor is found, the number is prime, and it is printed.

Here's an example of a for loop in C that generates and prints every prime number between 1 and 1000. The code uses a common approach known as the "Sieve of Eratosthenes" algorithm:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int i, j, primes[1001];

    // Initialize the primes array
    for (i = 2; i <= 1000; i++) {
        primes[i] = 1;
    }

    // Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm
    for (i = 2; i <= 1000; i++) {
        if (primes[i] == 1) {
            for (j = i * 2; j <= 1000; j += i) {
                primes[j] = 0;
            }
        }
    }

    // Print the prime numbers
    for (i = 2; i <= 1000; i++) {
        if (primes[i] == 1) {
            printf("%d ", i);
        }
    }

    return 0;
}
```

The time complexity of this code is approximately O(n log(log(n))), where n is the upper limit (1000 in this case). This is because the algorithm iterates through the numbers from 2 to n, applying the "Sieve of Eratosthenes" algorithm to mark non-prime numbers as 0. The inner loop runs approximately n/2 + n/3 + n/5 + ... times, resulting in a time complexity of O(n log(log(n))).

Note: The code provided is more efficient than a simple trial division approach, especially for larger ranges. The Sieve of Eratosthenes significantly reduces the number of iterations required to find prime numbers.

