        cv::imshow("Frame", frame);
        if (cv::waitKey(1) == 'q')
            break;
    }

    cap.release();
    cv::destroyAllWindows();

    return 0;
}
```

In this code, we use the OpenCV dnn module to load a pre-trained Keras CNN model saved in TensorFlow format (`.pb` file). The frame captured from the camera is preprocessed and fed as input to the loaded model. The predicted class label is obtained from the model's output, and it is then displayed on the frame. Pressing 'q' terminates the program.

Note that you need to replace `"model.pb"` with the correct path to your trained model file in TensorFlow format. Additionally, you may need to link OpenCV and its dnn module in your C++ project.

I hope this helps to get you started with using OpenCV and a Keras CNN model in C++!

For using a Keras CNN model in OpenCV, you can generally use any version of OpenCV as long as it supports the necessary modules and functionalities that you require. However, it is recommended to use OpenCV 4.x or later versions for better compatibility and access to the latest features.

The specific OpenCV modules required for loading and using deep learning models, such as TensorFlow models, are typically available in OpenCV's dnn module. In some cases, you may need to build OpenCV with the `WITH_DNN` and `WITH_TENSORFLOW` flags enabled to ensure the availability of these functionalities.

