import java.lang.reflect.Field;

class MyClass {
    private int myField;

    public MyClass(int myField) {
        this.myField = myField;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws NoSuchFieldException, IllegalAccessException {
        MyClass obj = new MyClass(10);

        // Get the field "myField" from the object
        Field field = obj.getClass().getDeclaredField("myField");

        // Allow access to private field
        field.setAccessible(true);

        // Get the value of the field for the object
        int value = (int) field.get(obj);

        System.out.println("Value of myField: " + value);
    }
}
```

In this example, we have a class `MyClass` with a private integer field `myField`. The `Main` class demonstrates how to use reflection to get the value of `myField` from an object of `MyClass`. We first get the `Field` object corresponding to the field name "myField" using the `getDeclaredField` method. We then use `setAccessible(true)` to allow access to the private field, and finally, we use `field.get(obj)` to retrieve the value of `myField`.

Running this code will output:
