In C#, the yield operator is used to define an iterator method that returns a sequence of values one at a time. It allows you to generate a sequence of values without having to construct and return a collection explicitly.

Java does not have a direct equivalent to the yield operator in C#, but you can achieve similar functionality using the Iterable interface, along with the Iterator and Iterable classes.

Here's an example of how you can implement a similar behavior to the yield operator in Java:

```java
import java.util.Iterator;

public class YieldExample implements Iterable<Integer> {
    private int start;
    private int end;

    public YieldExample(int start, int end) {
        this.start = start;
        this.end = end;
    }

    @Override
    public Iterator<Integer> iterator() {
        return new YieldIterator();
    }

    private class YieldIterator implements Iterator<Integer> {
        private int current;

        public YieldIterator() {
            current = start;
        }

        @Override
        public boolean hasNext() {
            return current <= end;
        }

        @Override
        public Integer next() {
            return current++;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        YieldExample example = new YieldExample(1, 5);
        for (int num : example) {
            System.out.println(num);
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, the YieldExample class implements the Iterable interface, indicating that it can be iterated over. The iterator() method returns an instance of the YieldIterator class, which implements the Iterator interface.

The YieldIterator class keeps track of the current value and implements the hasNext() and next() methods to control the iteration. The hasNext() method determines whether there are more values to yield, and the next() method returns the next value in the sequence.

Finally, in the main() method, we create an instance of the YieldExample class and iterate over it using a for-each loop. The loop will print the values 1 to 5, simulating the behavior of the yield operator in C#.

Note that this is just one way to achieve a similar behavior in Java, and there are other approaches and libraries available for implementing iterators and generators.

