The result will be a prompt to the user saying "Input: " with the cursor positioned immediately after it, and the user's input will be displayed on the next line.

Apologies for the confusion. If you want to display the "Input: " string and have the cursor positioned immediately after it on the same line without a newline, you can use the `System.out.print` method to print the string followed by a `System.out.flush()` call to ensure it is immediately displayed:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Input: ");
        System.out.flush(); // Flush the output to immediately display

        String input = scanner.nextLine();
        System.out.println("You entered: " + input);
    }
}
```

In this updated example, `System.out.flush()` is called after `System.out.print` to ensure that the text is immediately displayed on the console.

Now, when you run the program, you will see the prompt "Input: " on the same line as the cursor.

Here's an example of how you can create and manage an inventory system in Unity 2D:

First, you'll need to create a script called "Inventory" to manage the inventory items:

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;
using UnityEngine;

public class Inventory : MonoBehaviour
{
    public List<Item> items = new List<Item>();  // List to hold all the items
    public int inventorySpace = 12;  // Maximum number of items that can be held in the inventory

    // Method to add an item to the inventory
    public void AddItem(Item item)
    {
        if (items.Count < inventorySpace)
        {
            items.Add(item);
            Debug.Log(item.name + " added to inventory.");
        }
        else
        {
            Debug.Log("Inventory is full - cannot add " + item.name);
        }
    }

    // Method to remove an item from the inventory
    public void RemoveItem(Item item)
    {
        if (items.Contains(item))
        {
            items.Remove(item);
            Debug.Log(item.name + " removed from inventory.");
        }
        else
        {
            Debug.Log(item.name + " is not in the inventory.");
        }
    }
}
```

Next, create another script called "Item" to define the properties of each item:

```csharp
using UnityEngine;

[CreateAssetMenu(fileName = "New Item", menuName = "Inventory/Item")]
public class Item : ScriptableObject
{
    public new string name = "New Item";
    public Sprite icon = null;

    public virtual void Use()
    {
        // Code to use the item (e.g., open a door, activate a power-up, etc.)
        Debug.Log("Using " + name);
    }
}
```

Now, you can create individual items by right-clicking in Unity's Project window and selecting "Create -> Inventory -> Item". Customize each item's properties and icon as needed.

To add items to the inventory, you can write a script for an object that the player interacts with (e.g., a key or a pick-up):

```csharp
using UnityEngine;

public class PickupObject : MonoBehaviour
{
    public Item item;  // The item associated with this pickup

    private void OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D collision)
    {
        if (collision.CompareTag("Player"))
        {
            Inventory inventory = collision.GetComponent<Inventory>();
            if (inventory != null)
            {
                inventory.AddItem(item);
                Destroy(gameObject);  // Remove the pickup object from the scene
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Finally, attach the "Inventory" script to an empty game object in your scene, and attach the "PickupObject" script to relevant objects that you want the player to pick up.

This is a basic example to get you started. You can expand on this by creating more functions in the Inventory script, such as a method to check if the inventory contains a specific item, or to display the current inventory to the player.

Remember to also handle the UI elements (e.g., displaying inventory icons) according to your game's needs.

