Yes, that's correct. You can create an object `X` using a generative constructor, modify its state as desired, and then overwrite the field `F` with serialized data using the C++ Cereal library. 

Here's an example demonstrating how to achieve this:

```cpp
#include <cereal/archives/json.hpp>
#include <fstream>

class X {
public:
  int F;

  X(int f) : F(f) {}

  template<class Archive>
  void serialize(Archive& archive) {
      archive(F);
  }
};

int main() {
  // Create object X using a generative constructor
  X obj(42);
  std::cout << "Initially, F is: " << obj.F << std::endl;

  // Modify the object's state
  obj.F = 99;
  std::cout << "Modified F is: " << obj.F << std::endl;

  // Serialize the modified object to a file
  {
    std::ofstream file("serialized_data.json");
    cereal::JSONOutputArchive oarchive(file);
    oarchive(obj);
  }

  // Overwrite the field F with serialized data
  {
    std::ifstream file("serialized_data.json");
    cereal::JSONInputArchive iarchive(file);
    iarchive(obj);
  }

  std::cout << "After overwriting, F is: " << obj.F << std::endl;

  return 0;
}
```

In the above code, we create an object `obj` of type `X` using a generative constructor with an initial value of `F` as 42. Next, we modify the state of `obj` by changing the value of `F` to 99. Then, we serialize `obj` to a file named "serialized_data.json" using `cereal::JSONOutputArchive`. Later, we deserialize the data from the file and overwrite the field `F` in `obj` using `cereal::JSONInputArchive`.

After overwriting, you can observe that the value of `F` has been restored to the serialized data.

Don't forget to link against the Cereal library when compiling this code.

To send a GET request with JSON data using the `reqwest` crate in Rust, you can follow the code sample below:

First, make sure to add `reqwest` as a dependency in your `Cargo.toml`:

```toml
[dependencies]
reqwest = "0.11"
```

Then, in your code file, you can use the following code:

```rust
use reqwest::blocking::Client;
use serde::{Serialize};

#[derive(Serialize)]
struct RequestData {
    // Define your structure for JSON data here
    key1: String,
    key2: u32,
}

fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    // Create an instance of the reqwest client
    let client = reqwest::blocking::Client::new();

    // Create your JSON data
    let data = RequestData {
        key1: String::from("value1"),
        key2: 42,
    };

    // Convert the data to JSON
    let json_data = serde_json::to_string(&data)?;

    // Send the GET request with JSON data
    let response = client.get("https://api.example.com/endpoint")
        .header("Content-Type", "application/json")
        .body(json_data)
        .send()?;

    // Check the response status
    if response.status().is_success() {
        println!("GET request successful!");
    } else {
        println!("GET request failed: {}", response.status());
    }

    Ok(())
}
```

In this example, `RequestData` is a structure representing the JSON data you want to send in the GET request. You can modify it according to your data structure.

Make sure to replace `"https://api.example.com/endpoint"` with the actual URL endpoint you want to send the request to.

The code sets the `Content-Type` header to `"application/json"` and converts the JSON data to a string using `serde_json::to_string()`. Finally, it sends the GET request using the client's `send()` method.

Note that in this example, the request is made synchronously, blocking the execution until the request is complete. If you want to make asynchronous requests, you can use the `reqwest::Client` instead of `reqwest::blocking::Client` and utilize async/await syntax.

