Here's a sample Java code that calls a WSDL service in a Spring Boot application using Spring Web Services (SWS):

1. Add the necessary dependencies to your `pom.xml` file:

```xml
<dependencies>
    <!-- Other dependencies -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web-services</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>javax.xml.bind</groupId>
        <artifactId>jaxb-api</artifactId>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>
```

2. Create a configuration class to define the Spring bean for the web service template:

```java
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.ws.client.core.support.WebServiceGatewaySupport;
import org.springframework.ws.soap.client.core.SoapActionCallback;

@Configuration
public class WebServiceConfig {

    private static final String SERVICE_URL = "http://www.example.com/your_service.wsdl";

    @Bean
    public YourWebServiceTemplate yourWebServiceTemplate() {
        YourWebServiceTemplate webServiceTemplate = new YourWebServiceTemplate();
        webServiceTemplate.setDefaultUri(SERVICE_URL);

        return webServiceTemplate;
    }

    public static class YourWebServiceTemplate extends WebServiceGatewaySupport {

        public YourWebServiceResponse callWebService(YourWebServiceRequest request) {
            // Marshal the request object to XML
            // Example using JAXB:
            JAXBElement<YourWebServiceRequest> requestElement = new ObjectFactory()
                    .createYourWebServiceRequest(request);

            // Make the web service call
            JAXBElement<YourWebServiceResponse> responseElement = (JAXBElement<YourWebServiceResponse>) getWebServiceTemplate()
                    .marshalSendAndReceive(SERVICE_URL, requestElement,
                            new SoapActionCallback("http://www.example.com/your_service/your_method"));

            // Unmarshal the response XML to an object
            YourWebServiceResponse response = responseElement.getValue();

            return response;
        }
    }
}
```

3. Create the Web Service request and response classes using JAXB annotations:

```java
import javax.xml.bind.annotation.XmlRootElement;

@XmlRootElement(name = "YourWebServiceRequest")
public class YourWebServiceRequest {
    // Request fields and getters/setters
}

@XmlRootElement(name = "YourWebServiceResponse")
public class YourWebServiceResponse {
    // Response fields and getters/setters
}
```

4. Create a controller to handle the web service request:

```java
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PostMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestBody;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

@RestController
public class YourController {

    @Autowired
    private WebServiceConfig.YourWebServiceTemplate webServiceTemplate;

    @PostMapping("/your-service")
    public YourWebServiceResponse callWebService(@RequestBody YourWebServiceRequest request) {
        return webServiceTemplate.callWebService(request);
    }
}
```

In this example, a `YourWebServiceTemplate` class is created by extending `WebServiceGatewaySupport` from Spring Web Services. This class provides convenient methods for making web service calls and handling marshalling/unmarshalling of XML objects.

The `callWebService` method demonstrates how to send a request to the WSDL service and receive the response. The `DefaultUri` property is set to the WSDL location, and the `marshalSendAndReceive` method is used to send the request and receive the response. The `SoapActionCallback` provides the SOAP action required by the service method.

The controller class uses the `YourWebServiceTemplate` bean to handle incoming requests and make the web service call. The `@PostMapping` annotation is used to specify the endpoint and request method. The `@RequestBody` annotation is used to bind the request body to the `YourWebServiceRequest` object.

Note: Replace the placeholders like `YourWebServiceTemplate`, `YourWebServiceRequest`, `YourWebServiceResponse`, `http://www.example.com/your_service.wsdl`, etc., in the code with your own values according to your WSDL service.

