To implement a delayed queue in C#, you can use a combination of a priority queue and a timer. Here's an example implementation:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Threading;

// Define a class to represent an item in the queue
public class DelayedQueueItem<T>
{
    public T Item { get; set; }
    public DateTime ScheduledTime { get; set; }
}

// Create a delayed queue class
public class DelayedQueue<T>
{
    private readonly List<DelayedQueueItem<T>> queue;
    private Timer timer;

    public DelayedQueue()
    {
        queue = new List<DelayedQueueItem<T>>();
    }

    // Enqueue item with a delay in milliseconds
    public void Enqueue(T item, int delayMilliseconds)
    {
        var scheduledTime = DateTime.Now.AddMilliseconds(delayMilliseconds);

        lock (queue)
        {
            queue.Add(new DelayedQueueItem<T> { Item = item, ScheduledTime = scheduledTime });

            if (timer == null)
            {
                timer = new Timer(ProcessQueue, null, delayMilliseconds, Timeout.Infinite);
            }
        }
    }

    // Process the queue
    private void ProcessQueue(object state)
    {
        lock (queue)
        {
            var currentTime = DateTime.Now;
            var itemsToProcess = queue.Where(item => currentTime >= item.ScheduledTime).ToList();

            foreach (var item in itemsToProcess)
            {
                // Do something with the item
                Console.WriteLine("Processing item: " + item.Item);

                // Remove the item from the queue
                queue.Remove(item);
            }

            if (queue.Count > 0)
            {
                // Calculate the next scheduled time
                var nextScheduledTime = queue.Min(item => item.ScheduledTime);
                var delayMilliseconds = (int)(nextScheduledTime - currentTime).TotalMilliseconds;

                // Restart the timer with the new delay
                timer.Change(delayMilliseconds, Timeout.Infinite);
            }
            else
            {
                // No more items in the queue, dispose the timer
                timer.Dispose();
                timer = null;
            }
        }
    }
}

// Usage
public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        DelayedQueue<string> queue = new DelayedQueue<string>();

        // Enqueue items with delays
        queue.Enqueue("Item 1", 2000);
        queue.Enqueue("Item 2", 4000);
        queue.Enqueue("Item 3", 1000);
        queue.Enqueue("Item 4", 6000);

        // Wait for the items to be processed
        Thread.Sleep(8000);
    }
}
```

In this implementation, the `DelayedQueue<T>` class manages a list of `DelayedQueueItem<T>` objects. Each `DelayedQueueItem<T>` consists of an item of type `T` and the scheduled time at which it should be processed.

The `Enqueue` method adds an item to the queue with a specified delay in milliseconds. It calculates the scheduled time by adding the delay to the current time. If the timer is not already running, it starts the timer with the delay value.

The `ProcessQueue` method is called by the timer's callback. It checks the current time against the scheduled time of each item in the queue and processes the items that have reached their scheduled time. It removes the processed items from the queue. If there are more items in the queue, it calculates the next scheduled time and restarts the timer with the new delay. If the queue becomes empty, it disposes the timer.

In the `Main` method, we create an instance of `DelayedQueue<string>` and enqueue some items with different delays. We then wait for some time to allow the items to be processed. You can modify the delays and add logic in the `ProcessQueue` method to handle the processed items as per your requirements.

